WEBVTT

00:04.662 --> 00:07.012
Spillage has built up under the tail pulley of

00:07.012 --> 00:09.463
a crusher conveyor. It'll take ten minutes with

00:09.463 --> 00:12.850
a shovel. The belt is running, the crusher's running,

00:12.850 --> 00:15.700
and the shift is busy. This is exactly the moment

00:15.700 --> 00:18.725
when people get hurt on conveyors. Not during big

00:18.725 --> 00:21.887
repairs, but during small jobs that seem too quick

00:21.887 --> 00:23.738
to bother shutting down for.

00:24.852 --> 00:28.502
At Boonesboro in 2018, a worker had his hand on

00:28.502 --> 00:31.215
a tail pulley, reaching for set screws,

00:31.215 --> 00:33.727
while another employee turned that same pulley.

00:33.727 --> 00:36.227
Nobody hit a start button. The motion came from

00:36.227 --> 00:39.002
a person turning it by hand. The lesson from our

00:39.002 --> 00:41.777
own record is to lock out, tag out,

00:41.777 --> 00:44.165
and block motion before putting your hands near

00:44.165 --> 00:47.215
any pulley, even when it's being turned by hand.

00:48.342 --> 00:52.430
At Clover Bottom, in 2014 and 2017,

00:52.430 --> 00:55.167
drivers were hurt by the strain of using a pipe

00:55.167 --> 00:57.780
and a long bar to clear plugged bins.

00:57.780 --> 00:59.680
The lesson there was the same idea:

00:59.680 --> 01:02.667
a plugged bin or chute is a stop-and-follow-the-procedure

01:02.667 --> 01:06.542
job. Lock out, use approved tools, and never work

01:06.542 --> 01:08.005
beneath the material.

01:09.132 --> 01:13.220
Two standards set the rules. Under 30 CFR 56 and

01:13.220 --> 01:18.682
57.12016, electrically powered equipment is deenergized

01:18.682 --> 01:21.207
before mechanical work is done on it.

01:21.207 --> 01:23.608
Power switches are locked out, or other measures

01:23.608 --> 01:26.382
are taken, so the equipment can't be energized

01:26.382 --> 01:28.382
without the knowledge of the people working on

01:28.382 --> 01:31.058
it. Warning notices are posted at the switch and

01:31.058 --> 01:33.108
signed by the people doing the work.

01:34.218 --> 01:39.581
Under 56 and 57.14105, repairs or maintenance are

01:39.581 --> 01:42.618
done only after the power is off and the machinery

01:42.618 --> 01:44.818
is blocked against hazardous motion.

01:44.818 --> 01:47.780
Motion is allowed only when an adjustment or test

01:47.780 --> 01:50.293
can't be done without it, and then people must

01:50.293 --> 01:53.230
be effectively protected from it. For work on power

01:53.230 --> 01:58.730
circuits themselves, 56 and 57.12017 add the same

01:58.730 --> 02:01.368
lockout and warning-sign requirements.

02:01.368 --> 02:03.818
That work is for qualified electrical people,

02:03.818 --> 02:05.031
not for this job.

02:06.156 --> 02:09.406
One more thing before the steps. Under Part 48,

02:09.406 --> 02:12.768
section 48.7, you're task trained before you do

02:12.768 --> 02:15.856
a new task. Locking out a particular conveyor or

02:15.856 --> 02:18.531
crusher is part of that training, because every

02:18.531 --> 02:20.994
piece of equipment is a little different:

02:20.994 --> 02:23.369
different disconnects, different feeders,

02:23.369 --> 02:25.806
different stored energy. This video gives you the

02:25.806 --> 02:28.569
pattern. Your supervisor shows you how it applies

02:28.569 --> 02:30.981
to the equipment you'll actually work on.

02:30.981 --> 02:33.768
If you haven't been shown, you don't do it alone.

02:33.768 --> 02:34.856
Ask first.

02:38.182 --> 02:40.769
Step one is to plan and tell people.

02:40.769 --> 02:42.932
Before you touch anything, know what you're locking

02:42.932 --> 02:46.344
out. A crusher conveyor can have an electric drive,

02:46.344 --> 02:49.132
a crusher feeding it, other belts feeding or taking

02:49.132 --> 02:51.882
from it, hydraulics, and gravity acting on the

02:51.882 --> 02:54.982
belt and the rock on it. Then tell the plant operator

02:54.982 --> 02:57.407
and everyone affected that this conveyor is coming

02:57.407 --> 02:59.682
down and that you'll be working on it.

02:59.682 --> 03:02.195
Nobody should be surprised that a belt stopped,

03:02.195 --> 03:04.807
and nobody should restart it to find out why.

03:05.920 --> 03:09.345
Step two: shut the equipment down the normal way,

03:09.345 --> 03:12.445
with the regular controls, in the normal sequence.

03:12.445 --> 03:15.045
Stop the feed so material isn't dumping onto a

03:15.045 --> 03:17.832
stopped belt. Wait until everything has come to

03:17.832 --> 03:21.545
a complete stop. A big pulley or a crusher flywheel

03:21.545 --> 03:23.657
can keep turning for a while after the power is

03:23.657 --> 03:24.420
off.

03:25.559 --> 03:28.796
Step three: open the disconnect for this conveyor.

03:28.796 --> 03:31.659
A stop button or a pull cord is not lockout.

03:31.659 --> 03:33.934
A stop button can be overridden by an automatic

03:33.934 --> 03:37.358
control, a timer, or someone at another panel.

03:37.358 --> 03:39.284
Make sure you have the right disconnect.

03:39.284 --> 03:41.896
If the labels aren't clear, stop and ask.

03:41.896 --> 03:44.646
Locking out the wrong belt is the same as not locking

03:44.646 --> 03:45.534
out at all.

03:46.660 --> 03:49.847
Step four: put on your own lock and your own tag.

03:49.847 --> 03:52.460
One lock per person. If three people are working

03:52.460 --> 03:54.985
on that conveyor, three locks go on,

03:54.985 --> 03:57.835
usually on a hasp. Your key stays with you,

03:57.835 --> 04:00.285
in your pocket, not in the lock and not on the

04:00.285 --> 04:03.523
panel. Your tag says who you are and why the equipment

04:03.523 --> 04:06.072
is locked out. A lock you didn't put on yourself

04:06.072 --> 04:08.398
doesn't protect you. Your instructor will show

04:08.398 --> 04:11.298
you Allen's locks, tags and procedure.

04:12.430 --> 04:15.918
Step five: release or control stored energy.

04:15.918 --> 04:18.718
Turning off the power doesn't remove all of it.

04:18.718 --> 04:21.518
A tensioned belt and its take-up hold energy.

04:21.518 --> 04:24.643
An inclined belt loaded with rock can roll back.

04:24.643 --> 04:26.993
A hydraulic system can hold pressure.

04:26.993 --> 04:29.743
Material in a chute or bin can let go.

04:29.743 --> 04:32.230
Release the tension, relieve the pressure,

04:32.230 --> 04:34.880
and clear or secure material the way your procedure

04:34.880 --> 04:35.718
says.

04:36.845 --> 04:40.007
Step six: block against motion. This is the step

04:40.007 --> 04:43.545
the 2018 case is about. If a pulley can be turned

04:43.545 --> 04:46.920
by hand, by gravity, or by the weight of the belt,

04:46.920 --> 04:48.907
block it so it can't turn while your hands are

04:48.907 --> 04:51.632
near it. Lockout keeps the power off.

04:51.632 --> 04:53.357
Blocking keeps the parts still.

04:54.503 --> 04:57.740
Step seven: try to start it. With everyone clear,

04:57.740 --> 05:00.353
press start at the local controls. Nothing should

05:00.353 --> 05:03.053
move. If something does move, you locked out the

05:03.053 --> 05:06.165
wrong thing or missed a source. Stop and sort it

05:06.165 --> 05:08.790
out before anyone goes near the equipment.

05:08.790 --> 05:11.315
Then put the control back to off so it won't start

05:11.315 --> 05:13.178
the moment the power comes back.

05:16.508 --> 05:19.458
When the job's done, reverse the steps carefully.

05:19.458 --> 05:24.245
Put every guard back. Under 57.14107,

05:24.245 --> 05:27.433
moving parts such as head, tail and take-up pulleys

05:27.433 --> 05:30.495
must be guarded. Get your tools and blocking out.

05:30.495 --> 05:32.858
Make sure everyone is clear and knows the belt

05:32.858 --> 05:35.596
is about to start. Then each person removes his

05:35.596 --> 05:38.546
own lock and tag. Tell the operator the conveyor

05:38.546 --> 05:40.870
is ready, and start it the normal way.

05:41.990 --> 05:44.565
Never remove someone else's lock. The standard

05:44.565 --> 05:47.577
says locks are removed only by the person who installed

05:47.577 --> 05:50.627
them or by authorized personnel. If a lock is left

05:50.627 --> 05:53.190
on and the owner has gone home, you don't cut it

05:53.190 --> 05:56.553
off. Tell your supervisor. Authorized personnel

05:56.553 --> 05:58.965
will follow the mine's procedure, which starts

05:58.965 --> 06:01.690
with making sure that person really isn't on or

06:01.690 --> 06:03.002
near the equipment.

06:04.186 --> 06:07.449
Pull cords and emergency stops are for emergencies.

06:07.449 --> 06:09.936
Pull one if someone is caught or about to be.

06:09.936 --> 06:12.373
But a pull cord is not lockout, and it doesn't

06:12.373 --> 06:15.311
make a belt safe to work on. Know where they are.

06:15.311 --> 06:17.974
Pull cords are listed at Boonesboro on UC 1 through

06:17.974 --> 06:22.986
4, C2, 30, 31 and 32 and at the front and shop

06:22.986 --> 06:26.186
fuel tanks; at Clover Bottom on the Liwell plant,

06:26.186 --> 06:29.824
primary, secondary, pug mill, surge tunnel and

06:29.824 --> 06:33.236
recycle bin. At Dix River, the conveyors are guarded

06:33.236 --> 06:35.911
by railings along the travelways instead,

06:35.911 --> 06:41.436
which the standard allows under 56 and 57.14109.

06:41.436 --> 06:44.048
Never reach or climb past a railing while a belt

06:44.048 --> 06:46.748
is running, and lock out before any work.

06:47.894 --> 06:49.945
The same rules apply to the crusher.

06:49.945 --> 06:52.882
Allen's approved method for a bridged primary crusher

06:52.882 --> 06:55.294
is the hydraulic rock breaker first.

06:55.294 --> 06:57.544
If that doesn't clear it, the crusher is locked

06:57.544 --> 07:00.269
out and blocked before an excavator or blasting

07:00.269 --> 07:03.519
is used. Nobody climbs into a crusher or stands

07:03.519 --> 07:05.544
on a bridged rock to clear it.

07:06.693 --> 07:09.543
Here's a common one. A belt is running off to one

07:09.543 --> 07:12.493
side, and tracking it means watching it run.

07:12.493 --> 07:15.155
Can you work on a moving belt? Only for adjustments

07:15.155 --> 07:18.606
or tests that truly can't be done without motion,

07:18.606 --> 07:20.718
and only when you're effectively protected from

07:20.718 --> 07:23.455
the moving parts. That usually means making the

07:23.455 --> 07:26.518
adjustment from outside the guard, with tools designed

07:26.518 --> 07:28.981
for it, following a procedure you've been trained

07:28.981 --> 07:32.193
on. It never means reaching into a pinch point,

07:32.193 --> 07:34.205
removing a guard on a running belt,

07:34.205 --> 07:36.518
or cleaning near a pulley while it turns.

07:37.659 --> 07:40.308
The plant is down, trucks are lining up,

07:40.308 --> 07:42.546
and the operator calls on the radio asking how

07:42.546 --> 07:45.308
much longer. Somebody suggests pulling your lock

07:45.308 --> 07:47.321
for a minute to run some rock through,

07:47.321 --> 07:50.071
then locking it back out. The answer is no.

07:50.071 --> 07:52.409
Your lock stays on until your work is finished

07:52.409 --> 07:55.096
and you're clear. If the job will take longer,

07:55.096 --> 07:58.084
say so, and let the supervisor decide how to handle

07:58.084 --> 08:01.121
production. Nobody restarts around your lock,

08:01.121 --> 08:03.683
and you never leave your lock on and walk away

08:03.683 --> 08:05.233
without telling anyone.

08:06.344 --> 08:08.756
If a job carries over to the next crew,

08:08.756 --> 08:11.019
there should never be a moment when the equipment

08:11.019 --> 08:14.119
is unlocked. Follow your mine's procedure for handing

08:14.119 --> 08:16.756
it over, so the next person's protection is in

08:16.756 --> 08:18.719
place before yours comes off.

08:19.875 --> 08:22.750
Here's the sequence one more time. Tell people.

08:22.750 --> 08:25.487
Stop it the normal way. Open the disconnect.

08:25.487 --> 08:28.962
Put on your own lock and tag. Release stored energy.

08:28.962 --> 08:31.387
Block against motion. Try to start it.

08:31.387 --> 08:33.662
And when you're done, guards back on,

08:33.662 --> 08:37.025
everyone clear, and you remove only your own lock.

08:37.025 --> 08:39.800
Ten minutes of shoveling is not worth a hand.

08:40.916 --> 08:44.078
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

08:44.078 --> 08:45.578
override this video.
